<!doctype html>
<!--
  The app.

  **It cannot be a download page yet, and it says so rather than implying it.**
  Builds go to TestFlight's testers and none is on the App Store, so there is
  no App Store URL to link and no released version to name. `notYet` is the
  whole of the honest answer today.

  The Android question is answered here because it is the second thing somebody
  asks and the answer is short. It is deliberately not a promise.
-->
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1, viewport-fit=cover" />
    <title>Peace Garden — the app</title>
    <meta name="description" content="Peace Garden is for iPhone and iPad." />
    <meta name="color-scheme" content="dark light" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/site.css" />
    <link rel="icon" href="/assets/favicon.svg" type="image/svg+xml" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/assets/icon-180.png" />
  </head>

  <body>
    <div class="pool" aria-hidden="true"></div>

    <main class="page" id="page">
      <!--
        The bar. On every page of the site, and the only way between any two of
        them that is not a link somebody happened to put in a paragraph.
        `site.css` §The bar says why it is the masthead with two things added
        rather than a header of its own.
      -->
      <header class="masthead bar">
        <a class="bar__home" href="/">
          <picture>
            <source srcset="/assets/mark-light.svg" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
            <img class="mark" src="/assets/mark-dark.svg" alt="" width="44" height="44" />
          </picture>
          <span class="wordmark">Peace Garden</span>
        </a>
        <!--
          The ways on from here, as glyphs rather than words, so they read the
          same in every language: the garden (the app's own Garden mark,
          `GardenGlyph` in Chrome.swift), what the names mean (an open book,
          the app's `MeaningsGlyph`), and the language. Each glyph's words
          are its name and tooltip, written from the catalogue by the page's
          script. See site.css §The bar's glyphs.
        -->
        <span class="bar__ways">
          <a class="bar-glyph" href="/garden" data-s-label="gardenTitle"><svg class="bar-glyph__mark" viewBox="0 0 15 15" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M 4.674 4.059 C 3.796 1.407, 6.716 1.407, 5.838 4.059 C 8.089 2.405, 8.991 5.181, 6.198 5.166 C 8.467 6.795, 6.105 8.512, 5.256 5.850 C 4.407 8.512, 2.045 6.795, 4.314 5.166 C 1.521 5.181, 2.423 2.405, 4.674 4.059 Z M 5.784 14.100 C 5.652 10.932, 5.124 9.612, 5.256 7.500 M 10.140 14.100 C 9.678 10.932, 9.150 8.556, 8.622 6.180 M 10.140 14.100 C 10.338 10.404, 10.470 6.972, 10.338 3.540 M 10.140 14.100 C 10.800 10.932, 11.658 8.820, 12.318 6.972 M 4.200 14.100 L 11.328 14.100" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></a>
          <a class="bar-glyph" href="/meanings" data-s-label="meaningsTitle"><svg class="bar-glyph__mark" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M10 5.2C8.2 3.9 5.4 3.5 2.75 3.9V15.4C5.4 15 8.2 15.4 10 16.7C11.8 15.4 14.6 15 17.25 15.4V3.9C14.6 3.5 11.8 3.9 10 5.2ZM10 5.2V16.7" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg></a>
          <label class="bar-language">
            <span class="visually-hidden" id="language-label"></span>
            <svg class="bar-language__mark" viewBox="0 0 20 20" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M3 10a7 7 0 1 0 14 0a7 7 0 1 0 -14 0M10 3C7.4 5.3 7.4 14.7 10 17M10 3C12.6 5.3 12.6 14.7 10 17M3 10H17M4.2 6.2H15.8M4.2 13.8H15.8" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" /></svg>
            <span class="chooser__field">
              <select id="language" class="chooser__select"></select>
              <svg class="chooser__mark" viewBox="0 0 16 16" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M4 6.5 L8 10.5 L12 6.5" /></svg>
            </span>
          </label>
        </span>
      </header>

      <h1 class="heading" data-s="downloadTitle"></h1>

      <section class="about">
        <p class="body" data-s="downloadBody"></p>
        <p class="body" data-s="notYet"></p>
      </section>

      <!--
        What it runs on, and no version. Until 14 October 2026 this named
        "1.0 (1)", the first archive, which had gone stale by seven TestFlight
        builds without anybody touching the page (design/mass-test-2026-10-13/
        site/README.md, finding 6). A version belongs here once the App Store
        has one to name. The iOS it needs is `deploymentTarget` in
        project.yml, and `tools/deploy.sh --check` reads this line against it,
        so the two cannot drift apart unnoticed.
      -->
      <p class="notice body">iPhone and iPad · iOS 17 and later</p>

      <!--
        The foot: one hairline and the privacy notice. The language it used to
        hold went up into the bar, beside the other ways on from a page.
      -->
      <footer class="foot bar-foot">
        <a class="bar-foot__link" href="/privacy" data-s="privacyTitle"></a>
      </footer>
    </main>

    <script type="module" src="/assets/js/plain.js"></script>
  </body>
</html>
